            <ArticleTitle>Microvascular Decompression for Glossopharyngeal Neuralgia: Long-Term Follow-Up.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>151-156</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1878-8750(17)30279-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.wneu.2017.02.106</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine operative findings and outcome of microvascular decompression (MVD) for glossopharyngeal neuralgia (GPN). This research displayed the long-term outcomes of a large series of 35 cases with GPN treated with MVD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From January 2004 to June 2006, 35 consecutive patients were diagnosed with GPN. All of them underwent MVD. Demographic data, clinical presentation, operative findings, clinical results, operative complications were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 33 patients (94.3%) experienced complete pain relief immediately after MVD. Long-term follow-up was available for 30 of these 35 patients, and 28 of these 30 patients continued to be pain-free. There was no long-term operative morbidity in all cases. One patient had a cerebrospinal fluid leak and 1 case presented with delayed facial palsy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Classic GPN is usually caused by pulsatile neurovascular compression of the glossopharyngeal and vagus rootlets. MVD is a safe, effective, and durable operation for GPN.</AbstractText>
